text:
Interesting and dense read under 300 pages, this perspective of the world from 2020 is unsettling for an American reader! Book deals with international systems of governance (i.e. UN, G7, IMF) and concepts of individual nation's sovereignty and self-interest, liberalism vs authoritarian systems, internet technology and globalism, and economies. How the British / European empires and colonialism were followed by WWs and a post-war American hegemony for 70 years, now being aggressively challenged by Asian powers. 

The current multi-polar world where the old order is in disarray (US in many ways is skeptical of globalism as evidenced by Trump and MAGA, Russia is a mess, EU is weak, Japan is too old, UN has minimal influence), alternative structures remain unclear, with rapid emergence of technologies (e-commerce, social media, AI, green tech, cyber espionage, space weapons) with no clear consensus for governance; 

What is clear is that a unipolar hegemony will not be accepted (led either by the US or China, as Russia is not a credible player). Authors propose and explore an emergent and unique role for India as a rising economic power with a unique brand of a noisy democracy, situated between the east and the west, a traditional supporter of the UN, and is a full-throated counter-weight to China and a more predictable US ally.  The authors are scholars, and Shashi Tharoor was a former Under-Secretary General of the UN (lost out to Kofi  Annan?).

